A scene: The Storm and the Lawmen

Exterior. Virginia coast. Afternoon.

The wind comes hard off the Atlantic.

Walter Hensley stands in the surf, examining his line.

“One more cast.”

Cut to an empty road.

A tumbleweed rolls across the screen.

Except it’s not a tumbleweed. It’s a plastic grocery bag.

The camera pushes slowly toward two small dogs tied outside the general store.

NARRATOR: “By high noon, the lawmen had been abandoned at the edge of town.”

Marshal Scruff lifts his head.

Deputy Snuffles is licking a crack in the concrete.

NARRATOR: “Their supplies were dwindling. Their patience was thin. And the storm was closing in.”

A woman exits the store carrying a bag of ice.

“Aw, aren’t you two cute!”

She walks away.

The Marshal watches.

NARRATOR: “They had been offered no provisions.”

Cut back to the ocean.

Walter examines his empty hook.

“One more cast.”

Cut back to the dogs.

Their owner emerges from the store.

The Deputy loses his mind with excitement.

NARRATOR: “And at last, against impossible odds, the cavalry returned.”

The Marshal sneezes.

End scene.

How we build the universe

Here’s the mechanic I’d use.

1. Reality

Your photographs, encounters, conversations and observations

2. Pantheon Story Engine

Cultural anthropology · Character motives · Social dynamics · Mythic structure · Musical rhythm

3. Living World

Recurring characters · Locations · Relationships · Rivalries · Unresolved events · Changing circumstances

4. Stories

Short sketches · Mockumentaries · Episodic comedy · Drama · Folklore · Serialized adventures

We maintain a living canon ledger containing our fictional characters, observed inspirations, relationships, histories, plot threads, and changes over time. We keep what the photo actually establishes separate from the backstories we invent.

The really interesting part is that a background character in episode three could become the protagonist in episode twelve.

We don’t have to know that when we create them.

And we can shift the genre without changing the underlying world: your dogs can be Western gunslingers in one vignette, two ordinary family pets in another, and silent observers of a human comedy in a third.

We use the Pantheon’s delta calculus to track how each encounter changes relationships, its semantic overlays to discover unusual connections, and its narrative architecture to create setups and payoffs.

But we also allow unstructured moments. Some scenes exist simply because they’re beautiful or ridiculous.

I especially like giving you the role of the wandering observer. You move through the world living your actual life, gathering little fragments. We turn them into fictional lives that can intersect, diverge, and eventually produce stories neither of us planned at the beginning.

And one other creative rule: not everyone needs to be exceptional to be fascinating. A mundane character can become unforgettable through one tiny, perfectly observed habit.

Keep sending the photos. We can build Small Kingdoms as a continuing series, with The Eleven O’Clock Kingdom as one of its recurring little civilizations.

Eventually, we’ll have a whole town full of people who seem to have been living there long before we pointed the camera at them.